  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Bedside Page",
    "overview": "Longshelf is a reading place before it is an application. The current book leads, the other books follow as a quiet ledger, and the interface recedes once a page is open. Neutral grained paper, near-black ink and a single oxblood accent carry every screen, so the same place looks and behaves the same on a phone, a tablet and a laptop.\n\nLiterata gives titles and book text the voice of a printed book. Atkinson Hyperlegible Next handles the apparatus: labels, metadata, controls and progress. Structure comes from hairline rules, alignment and space. Book covers are the only objects that lift from the paper.\n\nThe system serves one neurodivergent owner in long, often low-light sessions. Stillness, plain words, visible focus and predictable placement are design materials here. The product name is Longshelf and the mark is the Bookend; the code's `--r-` token prefix remains from the working name until the separate codebase rename.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Paper, ink and one oxblood accent, in light, dark and Night.",
      "Literata for titles and reading; Atkinson Hyperlegible Next for apparatus.",
      "Hairlines and space instead of content cards.",
      "Covers carry the only soft shadows.",
      "State changes are instant, except the reader bars the owner summons.",
      "Every saved place is visible, and another device's place is offered, never applied silently."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Oxblood Rule",
        "body": "Oxblood means your place, a way onward, or the current state. Never use it as decoration, and never add a second accent.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Other Device Is Grey Rule",
        "body": "This device's progress is oxblood. Another device's progress is a neutral bar or tick, so the two places can never be confused.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Night Is Quieter Rule",
        "body": "Night removes the grain and lowers contrast from pure white while keeping reading text comfortably legible. Do not add texture or bright fills to it.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Voices Rule",
        "body": "Book names and titles speak in Literata. Controls and data speak in Atkinson.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Weight For Emphasis Rule",
        "body": "Interface emphasis uses weight (650), not italics or underline. Italics belong to the book's own typography, such as epigraphs and verse.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Natural Chapter Rule",
        "body": "A chapter heading keeps the book's natural size and spacing, in Literata at weight 500 and line height 1.15. The reader adds no label above it.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Progress Rule",
        "body": "Percentages and counts in the interface use tabular figures so they do not jitter between rows.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Book Leads Rule",
        "body": "On Home, the current book comes first. Do not put a grid, dashboard or stat above it.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Same Place Rule",
        "body": "Navigation, tabs and reader tools keep their order and position on every device and visit.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Only Books Lift Rule",
        "body": "Do not use shadows on content sections or rows. If it is not a cover or a temporary overlay, it stays on the paper.",
